<h3>Why routing is needed &mdash; AI cannot find what it does not know the location of</h3>
<p>You have surely had the experience of the AI asking back <strong>"please give me more information"</strong> even though the file is clearly in the project. That is usually because <strong>you never told it the file was there</strong>.</p>
<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">The misconception &mdash; "it will dig through everything on its own"</span>
    AI does <strong>not automatically dig through the whole codebase</strong> (doing that would only waste time and tokens). If it <strong>does not know</strong> something is somewhere, <strong>it cannot find it.</strong> With routing done right, <strong>you no longer need to repeat the same explanation</strong> &mdash; you just speak, and it knows where to look.
</div>

<h3>Folders the way you understand them</h3>
<p>There is <strong>still no settled standard</strong> for the correct folder structure. The way someone else does it is not the only right answer. There is one criterion &mdash; <strong>does it make sense to you, and does it make sense to your AI too.</strong></p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Folder example (can also be organized by date)</span>
context/
  about-me.md          <span class="c"># Me and how I work (background that is always true)</span>
  stack.md
  decisions.md         <span class="c"># Decision log — add an entry with the date on big changes</span>
projects/
  youtube/2026-05-30-top50.md
  clients/acme.md</code></pre>

